"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2,4H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanal", "common_name": "beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h3H,1-2,4H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H8N2", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile", "common_name": "proline aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "DL-proline", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "L-proline", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Discharge fixation of methane and nitrogen to hydrogen cyanide and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["electric discharge", "silicate surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K bulk; plasma microzones much hotter", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silicate surface", "aqueous": false, "other": "Reducing CH4/N2 gas phase or gas-water interface; best treated as local/transient rather than global atmosphere chemistry."}, "mechanism": "Electrical discharge fragments CH4 and N2 into radicals; recombination yields HCN and NH3.", "reasoning": "Reduced-gas discharge experiments on CH4/N2/H2O with traces of NH3 generated the HCN-rich chemistry from which amino acids, including proline, arise (Ring et al., 1972). HCN is also photochemically plausible in some N2-rich atmospheres (Rugheimer and Rimmer, 2019), and silicate/glass surfaces alter discharge product distributions (Criado-Reyes et al., 2021). The written stoichiometry is a lumped source step, not a claimed elementary process; a globally CH4-rich atmosphere remains contested (Trail et al., 2011)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Energetic methane-water conversion to formaldehyde and hydrogen", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["electric discharge or UV", "silicate surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K bulk; energetic microzones much hotter", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silicate surface", "aqueous": false, "other": "Speculative source-consolidation step for carbonyl formation and reducing-equivalent generation at a gas-water interface."}, "mechanism": "Energetic activation of methane and water gives oxygen insertion to formaldehyde and release of H2.", "reasoning": "This is explicitly speculative as a source-consolidation node: the supplied proline papers use formaldehyde as a feedstock rather than isolating the exact net reaction written here. It is included because reduced-gas discharge chemistry does generate carbonyl species (Ring et al., 1972; Criado-Reyes et al., 2021), and formaldehyde is a recurrent precursor in both the Fox-Windsor and Hennet proline route families (Fox and Windsor, 1970; Hennet et al., 1992)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanovinylation of acetylene to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light", "HS-", "Cu(I)/Cu(II)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Cyanosulfidic shallow pond or evaporitic pool with UV irradiation."}, "mechanism": "Cyanovinylation of acetylene gives acrylonitrile under photoredox conditions.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) place acrylonitrile at the entry point of the proline branch of cyanosulfidic chemistry. This is the most mechanistically explicit proline-specific route in the supplied literature, with UV/sulfide/Cu chemistry used to manage nitrile formation under surface conditions (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich surface pool."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia undergoes conjugate addition to the activated alkene/nitrile.", "reasoning": "Ammonia addition to acrylonitrile to give beta-aminopropionitrile was directly tracked in the Patel network, with high conversion reported. Unlike the older discharge and thermal routes, this intermediate is experimentally identified rather than merely inferred (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Reduction of beta-aminopropionitrile to 3-aminopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light", "HS-", "Cu(I)/Cu(II)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II)", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV photoreduction; H2 is used here as a net reducing-equivalent placeholder for sulfide-driven reduction."}, "mechanism": "Net photoreductive conversion of the nitrile to an aldehyde, with hydrolytic loss of the nitrile nitrogen as NH3.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) experimentally converted beta-aminopropionitrile to 3-aminopropanal under UV-driven sulfide chemistry. In the balanced net written here, H2 is a bookkeeping proxy for the reducing equivalents delivered experimentally by HS- photoredox and geochemically by strongly reducing fluids; the stoichiometry is condensed, but the intermediate identity is literature-supported (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cyanohydrin-thioamide cyclization to proline aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light", "HS-", "Cu(I)/Cu(II)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Compressed representation of cyanohydrin, thioamide, cyclization, and reductive cyanation chemistry; H2 again denotes net reducing equivalents."}, "mechanism": "3-Aminopropanal forms a cyanohydrin, enters thioamide/cyclic thioamide chemistry, cyclizes, and is reductively cyanated to the cyclic aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) mapped 3-aminopropanal through cyanohydrin and thioamide chemistry, cyclization to a gamma-butyrothiolactam manifold, and final cyanide chemistry to a cyclic alpha-aminonitrile precursor of proline. This node compresses that experimentally supported manifold into one balanced net transformation while retaining the key structural anchors 3-aminopropanal and proline aminonitrile; environmental execution remains demanding because UV, cyanide, sulfide, and careful sequencing are all required (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of proline aminonitrile to rac-proline", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Prolonged aqueous heating or wet-dry/geothermal cycling; stoichiometrically two waters are implied."}, "mechanism": "Aqueous nitrile hydrolysis converts the cyclic alpha-aminonitrile to rac-proline.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) stop at the cyclic aminonitrile rather than quantifying free proline from the same sequence. Hydrolysis of alpha-aminonitriles to amino acids is chemically standard, so this step is plausible, but in the context of the Patel route it remains partly extrapolative rather than directly measured (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrothermal HCN-formaldehyde synthesis of rac-proline", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005", "mol_013"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage", "illite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "420-430K", "pressure": "10 bar", "catalyst": "pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage or illite", "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN/HCHO/NH4+-rich reducing flow with rapid quench favored over long high-temperature residence."}, "mechanism": "Hot cyanide-carbonyl chemistry on Fe-S/clay minerals yields amino acids; proline emerges through unresolved network cyclization.", "reasoning": "Hennet et al. (1992) reported proline among amino acids formed from HCN/HCHO/NH4+ at about 150 C and 10 bar with pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite or illite. The present node is a balanced summary of that complex hydrothermal network rather than a resolved elementary mechanism; the authors did not claim their high precursor concentrations were natural, and amino-acid survival is strongly limited by temperature and residence time (Hennet et al., 1992; Bada et al., 1995; Yanagawa and Kobayashi, 1997)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia synthesis of rac-proline precursors", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "450-460K", "pressure": "sealed / moderate", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Thermal HCHO/NH3 condensate; experimentally the analyte was released after hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Thermal carbonyl-ammonia condensation produces hydrolysable N-rich organics that release proline on hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Fox and Windsor (1970) observed proline only after hydrolysis of a heated HCHO/NH3 product mixture. This node is therefore a balanced lumped representation of high-temperature aqueous condensation chemistry leading to proline-releasing material, not a directly observed one-step free-proline synthesis; hydrolysis-based assays require caution, as emphasized in related early work (Fox and Windsor, 1970; Wolman et al., 1971)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Direct Miller-type discharge synthesis of rac-proline", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["electric discharge", "silicate surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K bulk; plasma microzones much hotter", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silicate surface", "aqueous": false, "other": "Strongly reducing discharge chemistry; experimentally proline was a minor product."}, "mechanism": "Plasma radical chemistry generates a complex HCN/carbonyl-rich mixture from which a small proline branch emerges.", "reasoning": "Ring et al. (1972) directly detected proline in electric-discharge products from CH4/N2/H2O with trace NH3, but at low abundance and without a resolved ring-forming mechanism. This node encodes that route family as a balanced overall branch; surface effects on Miller-type chemistry are real, and a globally reducing atmosphere is disputed, so this is best treated as local/transient surface chemistry (Ring et al., 1972; Criado-Reyes et al., 2021; Trail et al., 2011)."}, {"id