xn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.54,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.71,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.39,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.28,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.48,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the most fully sourced Patel variant, but also the most environmentally under-justified because discharge products and methane-water source chemistry are fed into a surface cyanosulfidic sequence without explicit deposition or concentration steps. rxn_002 needs the 2 H2 stoichiometry, and rxn_006 still omits the essential sulfur and photoredox part of the published mechanism. L-proline is not actually established because rxn_011 is unresolved.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Insert explicit transfer and concentration nodes linking atmospheric source chemistry to the surface cyanosulfidic environment.\",\n        \"Correct rxn_002 and replace rxn_006 with explicit sulfur-bearing Patel intermediates.\",\n        \"Keep the pathway target at DL-proline unless an evidenced enantioenrichment mechanism is added.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.61,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.40,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.58,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.53,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.25,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.44,\n      \"feedback\": \"Reasonable as a hydrothermal route-family summary to rac-proline, since HCN and formaldehyde hydrothermal experiments do report minor proline. However rxn_008 is not a mechanistic one-step synthesis and should be interpreted only as an overall node; the balanced net form is 3 HCN + 6 CH2O -> C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3 + 4 CO. The literature also uses NH4+ and reducing minerals at about 150 C and 10 bar, which are absent here, and rxn_011 does not solve L-selectivity.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Represent rxn_008 explicitly as an HCN and HCHO and NH4+ and mineral hydrothermal route-family node rather than a single resolved reaction.\",\n        \"Correct the rxn_008 stoichiometry and note the mineral and pressure context from the literature.\",\n        \"Keep the target at DL-proline unless an evidenced L-enrichment process is supplied.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.61,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.42,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.57,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.48,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.25,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.43,\n      \"feedback\": \"Adding rxn_001 gives a plausible HCN source, but there is still no explicit deposition or concentration step from atmospheric discharge into the hydrothermal reactor. rxn_008 remains an overall route-family node rather than a resolved mechanism, and rxn_011 is speculative. This is better aligned with the literature as a sourced rac-proline pathway than as a route to L-proline.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add deposition and concentration of atmospheric HCN into the hydrothermal setting.\",\n        \"Add NH4+ and mineral redox buffers to the rxn_008 description and correct its stoichiometry.\",\n        \"Separate L-enrichment from synthesis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.72,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.57,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.61,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.44,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.56,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.44,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.25,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.42,\n      \"feedback\": \"Structurally complete, but environmentally weakest of the hydrothermal variants because both discharge and methane-water source modules are piped into hydrothermal chemistry without an explicit transport or concentration step. Correct rxn_002 and rxn_008 stoichiometries, add NH4+ and mineral context to rxn_008, and keep L-enrichment separate from synthesis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_002 and rxn_008 stoichiometries.\",\n        \"Insert transfer and concentration steps from atmospheric source chemistry to hydrothermal fluids.\",\n        \"Do not treat rxn_011 as established prebiotic chemistry.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.47,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.31,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.47,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.42,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.22,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.35,\n      \"feedback\": \"Supported only at the route-family level. Fox-Windsor type chemistry gives proline mainly after hydrolysis of heated HCHO and NH3 products, so rxn_009 overstates the evidence by making free DL-proline directly. The balanced overall form is 6 CH2O + NH3 -> C5H9NO2 + CO + 3 H2O, but a proline-releasing precursor node would align better with the literature; rxn_011 remains unsupported.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Change rxn_009 to a proline-releasing precursor or hydrolysate node, not direct free proline.\",\n        \"Correct rxn_009 stoichiometry and move the environment to a thermal aqueous or evaporative setting if that is what is intended.\",\n        \"Separate L-enrichment from synthesis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.57,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.47,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.35,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.46,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.39,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.22,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.34,\n      \"feedback\": \"Adding rxn_002 does not fix the main issue with the thermal route, which is that the literature supports hydrolysable precursor mixtures more clearly than direct free proline. rxn_002 needs CH4 + H2O -> CH2O + 2 H2, rxn_009 needs the 6 to 1 stoichiometry, and an environmental link between source chemistry and the thermal condensation setting is missing. L-proline is still not achieved.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_002 and rxn_009 stoichiometries.\",\n        \"Add a transfer or concentration step linking methane-water source chemistry to the thermal condensation setting.\",\n        \"Represent the product as a hydrolysable precursor mixture unless direct free-proline evidence is supplied.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.64,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.08\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.41,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.60,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.48,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.25,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.45,\n      \"feedback\": \"Direct discharge has real literature support for racemic proline in complex product mixtures, so this is a defensible overall route-family node. Still, rxn_010 is not a resolved mechanism, should be balanced as 5 CH4 + 3 N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + 5 NH3, and closer literature alignment would mention trace NH3, low proline yield, and the debated reducing atmosphere. rxn_011 remains an unsupported resolution step.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Keep rxn_010 as a low-yield discharge route-family node and correct its stoichiometry.\",\n        \"Specify atmospheric discharge conditions and note the reducing-atmosphere limitation from the literature.\",\n        \"Treat L-resolution as unresolved rather than achieved.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.59,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"Network validity is good: the graph is connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and the supplied balance check reports no true conservation violations. However six reactions need explicit coefficients: rxn_002 CH4 + H2O -> CH2O + 2 H2; rxn_006 C3H7NO + 2 HCN + 2 H2 -> C5H8N2 + NH3 + H2O; rxn_007 C5H8N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + NH3; rxn_008 3 HCN + 6 CH2O -> C5H9NO2 + 2 NH3 + 4 CO; rxn_009 6 CH2O + NH3 -> C5H9NO2 + CO + 3 H2O; rxn_010 5 CH4 + 3 N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H9NO2 + 5 NH3. Literature alignment is fair to good: the network correctly treats Patel's branch as the most explicit proline-specific route and acknowledges that abiotic L-selective proline synthesis is not established. The main chemical weakness is rxn_006, which invokes thioamide chemistry without any sulfur species and collapses the documented HS-, UV, and Cu sequence too aggressively; rxn_005 is also under-specified. The hydrothermal, thermal, and discharge nodes are acceptable only as route-family summary nodes, with rxn_009 overstating the literature because Fox-Windsor mainly observed proline after hydrolysis of thermal products. Environmental consistency is mixed because atmospheric discharge steps are labeled Surface, hydrothermal conditions omit NH4+ and mineral buffers, and rxn_007 and rxn_011 